> Oh I forgot to mention: I'm well aware of the benefits of elven
> daggers (they do a whole point of damage more on average in comparison
> to orcish ones against small creatures, and they don't rust or
> corrode) but I couldn't get a stack of them much bigger than 4 or 5.

Elves are common in the middle game.  If you have seen the Quest portal
you are now seeing elves regularly.  Time will supply you with a stack
of a dozen daggers.  You can start enchanting the stack at that point. 
No hurry.

> They also have the penalty of rotting in water and burning in fire, so
> I was planning on enchanting the regular daggers instead and ditching
> the orcish ones once I got them enchanted to +6 or +7

Regular daggers rust the same as orcish.  Either type need to be
rendered rustproof at some point if you wish to use them against any
sort of monster.  Pick and choose when to use them, not quite against
everything.

Silver daggers are inherently rust free but far too rare for most
characters to ever get a sizable stack.  They are the best stackable
dagger type but their rarity makes their quality pointless.

It's my experience that it is not easy to burn an elven dagger.  Blasts
of fire seem to damage my eligable armor not my eligable weapons and
that seems to be the primary reason for the difference.  Not a great
idea to poke a brown pudding with an elven dagger, though, just like
it's not a great idea to poke a black pudding with an iron dagger. 
Unless you deliberately want splits.  Still, by the time I've gotten
below the Castle my characters who can get expert in dagger tend to have
a dozen blessed fireproof +4 or better elven daggers.

